'''view''' (vū), ''v.'' [Early mod. E. also ''vewe''; < ''view'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To see; look on; behold.
{{smaller block|<poem>When most I wink, then do mine eyes best see,
For all the day I ''view'' things unrespected.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, xliii.}}
<poem>The people ''view’d'' them wi’ surprise,
As they danc’d on the green.</poem>
{{right|''The Earl of Mar’s Daughter'' (Child’s Ballads, I. 177).}}}}
'''2.''' To examine with the eye; look on with attention, or for the purpose of examining; survey; explore; peruse.
{{smaller block|Go up and ''view'' the country. {{right|Josh. vii. 2.}}
<poem>Lords, ''view'' these letters full of bad mischance.
France is revolted from the English quite.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., i. 1. 89.}}
I had not the opportunity to ''view'' it.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 137.}}
<poem>For he ''viewed'' the fashions of that land;
Their way of worship ''viewed'' he.</poem>
{{right|''Young Beichan and Susie Pye'' (Child’s Ballads, IV. 2).}}}}
'''3.''' To survey intellectually; examine with the mental eye; consider; regard.
{{smaller block|As Princes be more high and also mightier than the rest, euen so are they more behelde & also more ''viewed'' than others.
{{right|''Guevara'', Letters (tr. by Hellowes, 1577), p. 10.}}
<poem>And though, oft looking backward, well she ''vewede''
Her selfe freed from that foster insolent.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., III. iv. 50.}}
When we ''view'' an object as a concrete whole we apprehend it. {{right|''J. Sully'', Outlines of Psychol., p. 331.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 1.''' To witness.—'''2.''' To scan.—'''3.''' To contemplate.}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To look; take a view. [Rare.]

